示例：(来自PISA sim/seen/01)
Generate a video that showcase the following scene: Create a 4‑second low-angle midground shot of a single blue sneaker moving from right → center-left across the frame. Camera: ground-level perspective, slight three-quarter view so the shoe's outer side and sole edge are visible; background should be out of focus and unobtrusive (nighttime plaza, soft lights) — focus entirely on the shoe's motion.

Motion profile:
Horizontal: steady, constant-speed translation from off-screen right to center-left across the midplane.
Vertical: rhythmic up-and-down oscillation (bobbing) that repeats evenly — about 2 bounces per second. Peak lift is small: the shoe rises ~20–30% of its own height above its lowest point.
Contact: the shoe never fully flattens on the ground — it hovers slightly, then briefly 'lands' close to the surface before rebounding; treat landings as soft, bounce-like impulses rather than hard stomps.
Rotation / attitude: small pitch around the lateral axis — toe tilts up on ascent and down on descent (±15–25°); add a subtle yaw shift (≤10°) so the visible side and sole edge change slightly as it moves; no full spin.
Shadow sync: include a ground shadow that scales and darkens as the shoe descends (shadow smallest and faintest at apex, largest and darkest at lowest point) to read altitude clearly.

Duration: 4 s
Horizontal travel: ~60–70% of frame width (from off-screen right to center-left)
Vertical amplitude: ~0.2–0.3 × shoe height
Pitch range: ±15–25°, yaw ≤10°

